{:edb/db-before-t 2234 :edb/db-after-t 2235 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535547 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:17:17.491Z" 13194139535547 true] [17592186228891 1100 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above.  What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" 13194139535547 true] [17592186228891 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $[MATH: x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above. What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" 13194139535547 false] [17592186228891 1145 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x)\n\\sim\n\\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2}\n+\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\Big(\n(\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx)\n+\n(\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx)\n\\Big).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-3a_n+4c_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-3b_n+4d_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$." 13194139535547 true] [17592186228891 1145 "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x) \\sim \\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\Big((\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx) + (\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx) \\Big)\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n- $-3a_n+4c_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n- $-3b_n+4d_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$." 13194139535547 false] [17592186228892 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$. Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139535547 false] [17592186228892 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$ Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." 13194139535547 true] [17592186228892 1145 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\cos (2(-x)) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (-2x) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (2x) \\\\\n&=-f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nwe have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0$. Thus, $a_n=0$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\ge1\n$$\n\nand $a_0=0$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139535547 false] [17592186228892 1145 "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) = -f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0.$ Thus, $a_n=0$ for all $n\\ge1,$ and $a_0=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms  in its Fourier series must be zero." 13194139535547 true] [17592186228893 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/238174bfcd0e07603a8516f9271efff876e36cf8021403be57b049ec31b60977.png)\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/a600da93d54ed8b4857befa58c1165e8c02b77d1c2fc5b1f460961dc135c50a7.png) be the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/1898332940848e9192873afe50ec9d9a005baf8bcb86ce77c66231321ba3fe0e.png)-periodic extension of a function from ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/5ff1e4e25630b889a7d37b5ac7252742b9b0f0abdf659ece1f3b0700815ddc44.png) to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/d33b55bb712e2f8437446299936998a79ee357a6aa77ce6c31932bb464868bb8.png) with the Fourier series shown above. What is the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/db700c8ee08453d9f4b0f347c4f88daf4186495494840905e9196b2243899653.png)th sine Fourier coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/c38b7489bfb362de743d5a53941eaddfc382f5d50756b13bc6a4fda893a692e9.png)" 13194139535547 true] [17592186228893 1100 "$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)$\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extesion of a function from $[MATH: x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th sine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)$?" 13194139535547 false